{# What you are most likely to be sitting in, and how often.

   Flights, never airframes. This page has no idea how many aircraft an airline
   owns; it knows how many of its upcoming departures are flown by each type,
   which is a different fact and the one a passenger is actually asking. The
   note says "flights" on every chip so the number cannot be read as a fleet
   count.

   Ordered by that count, which is the whole reason the block is worth drawing:
   every airline in this data flies all 28 types, so the list is only
   interesting in its order -- and the order is set by how long the airline's
   legs are, because an aircraft is chosen by whether its range covers the leg.
   See AirlineRepository::aircraft().

   Widebodies are marked. It is the one attribute of an aircraft a passenger
   feels -- two aisles, and a long flight -- and it is what makes the
   difference between two airlines visible at a glance rather than only to
   somebody who knows what a Dash 8 is. #}
<ul class="chips">
    {% for type in aircraft %}
        <li>
            <span class="chip{{ type.is_widebody ? ' chip--wide' : '' }}">
                <i class="fas {{ type.is_widebody ? 'fa-plane' : 'fa-plane-up' }} chip__icon"
                   aria-hidden="true"></i>
                {{ type.title }}
                <span class="chip__note">{{ type.flights|number_format }} flights</span>
            </span>
        </li>
    {% endfor %}
</ul>

<p class="chips__note">Counted over departures in the next {{ window_days }} days.</p>
